#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39d79985-57fa-554a-b697-4bd6dd71cbe2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "fc21ef44-25b7-5157-bbdb-c1284d1aa7ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-6254" :question/problem "Consider the matrix ![](images/fb/fb428b64d8d1fc020f343aea11f50ad92158ad4dcbf611b5decb44bd79ac221c.svg) Find a basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we need reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/7a/7abb144e511574809625c59d5ed6b7658983fc646bd0e6d6327f9f12356b8f08.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/id #uuid "302059c1-d807-54d2-badc-a4a19b2f9c05" :question/math-academy-id "e-6255" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/b2/b22e2ffa6f6d88665e120e8dad82961e72f8f4a99484e8f81de311a66ab25988.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ \nIf that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_3=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/28/28f79983a195fa0152832aebd334cd8e1aaaa977dd9016347a3152403fd390c7.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](images/45/4588c7d7ae4fd6cbc749373d83ab2d81e6a228f0cac03d5b959f4177f3f4cdf9.svg)\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/fa/fa2ad666a6b80eac26bad12db322fa361578d94327e230d7c520c950add78923.svg)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 5 \\cdot 2=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151945 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "d5bcbec9-0b72-5ba1-ad84-c770cf3a0c0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6272" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$,  $\\mathbf{a}_3$, and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/af/afbd6ce90f2192c311c38ced9c1d9512c48bbf9a5e06236415ef9fe70883f9be.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2ccba053acf8d050e305c3768c970ae635f4583163a867b6ef11f4e565ac1ae.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -12 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n![](images/6b/6b4306172b78c1e59bf66c8c08bf864aac2bcab65d4b1cc08ba4ddab0e5f1da3.svg)\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_4$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/b3/b3681d7f6c71d06ede32616ceec12ccd71da28373ee88eab451211af5a579212.svg)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 2 \\cdot (-3) = -6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1640f98f-b934-544d-b764-303053864f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244517} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7e1df3-4580-5975-b457-6c0295c1f461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244591} {:answer/id #uuid "53145b12-05ac-544a-99da-924b20f9b963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245047} {:answer/id #uuid "5891d6a5-5899-58e5-aad9-238b06855faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245097} {:answer/id #uuid "ecfbaa5c-8266-5fb2-9b94-fdf08e2337ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecfbaa5c-8266-5fb2-9b94-fdf08e2337ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246409} :answer-field/id #uuid "18d39719-9109-5df8-9dbf-ca0c2b5cad6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1560ce17-f00a-5fcc-81e8-d48fb2971bfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-111468" :question/problem "The matrix $D$ is given below. Let ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, and ${d}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $D.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(D).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${d}_{2}={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{3}{d}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is $\\{{d}_{1},{d}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${d}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{2}}={{(-6)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=-6\\cdot 0=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5750330-79d7-50e2-9ded-15a59101eba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149982} {:answer/id #uuid "b785a5e7-ecc7-5455-a788-080e518def4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149983} {:answer/id #uuid "c9377f2f-9488-5514-aeeb-48e23c16df47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149984} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe9633c-3b72-5df8-adc7-1006f8331422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149986} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8dfba9-883e-5552-a0f0-cb69cf694e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fe9633c-3b72-5df8-adc7-1006f8331422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149986} :answer-field/id #uuid "60a2d415-9e9a-5c72-aafa-7f2b41592aa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c555c8c9-de13-50dd-99c1-9c9975c94651" :question/math-academy-id "q-34462" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and ${a}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{(-1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-1)\\cdot 3=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39d79985-57fa-554a-b697-4bd6dd71cbe2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa456ff-14e6-5b4c-8bb6-5fa53d7a73e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423601} {:answer/id #uuid "1b239761-1533-5b95-9abe-da3e7f8edb7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423602} {:answer/id #uuid "2b80385e-3a37-5b43-b815-d32cf7c3607c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423603} {:answer/id #uuid "79611b73-f06c-516d-a314-79fc8a0df50a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423607} {:answer/id #uuid "ce207c25-42d3-5952-b1ac-04aeb42aad0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b239761-1533-5b95-9abe-da3e7f8edb7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423602} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb5ef360-4ae2-56e4-8b56-8bb54c7ef0fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28419572-5f5f-5d91-aa00-334d8b1c45ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-34514" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$F=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(F).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(F),$ we reduce the matrix $F$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 19\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$rd columns from the original matrix are ${f}_{1}$ and ${f}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39d79985-57fa-554a-b697-4bd6dd71cbe2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ef60749-f0a3-5b92-b7ac-687d57c40bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423608} {:answer/id #uuid "842c1b53-7a3c-5c3c-95cd-f635e53b9e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423609} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1ff8c3-beef-5403-bf54-dedcfd80e6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423610} {:answer/id #uuid "c1363c60-15a5-5bca-ae79-3b740d941738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423613} {:answer/id #uuid "c62c345c-75b2-5956-a3af-45a35e4a85f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f1ff8c3-beef-5403-bf54-dedcfd80e6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423610} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d734853-a228-557f-9012-a5d01ef1cf52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c2bc939-4b4c-5161-9351-90818be5a0a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75070" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(V).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(V),$ we reduce the matrix $V$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3214ac66-089e-5f1a-b5f5-27127d89af81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342662} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6f8a38-0a5a-5b8d-9bbd-8d6b55a7767b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343032} {:answer/id #uuid "69df0ecf-34a7-5ab2-a85a-47fd55e5f70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343142} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9fef81-c774-530d-bce3-3ec15ebb79e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344469} {:answer/id #uuid "f635b87e-9ad6-595e-8ac8-cf7446521d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f635b87e-9ad6-595e-8ac8-cf7446521d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423617} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab78a2e2-73c6-516c-bb41-33e676435ed4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c466a2ff-d594-54f8-8655-5aeb347fd10d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75980" :question/problem "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${v}_{2}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e3091b-8980-52e7-a1d9-a90be4aa96ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423604} {:answer/id #uuid "3d86af19-6bb9-5da7-832b-79a566c84f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423605} {:answer/id #uuid "67c52e20-2bf3-5d92-86ef-d45fa30e8513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423606} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2e8bb6-fedc-5092-b791-68f16dc74270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423611} {:answer/id #uuid "a9845672-a026-53af-b2ba-1e8d7af5f91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35e3091b-8980-52e7-a1d9-a90be4aa96ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423604} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f4cbcc-1506-5396-b835-9d7d9a6a3ab8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "693e6ed3-909b-52a6-8fa3-e0e6ae013327" :question/math-academy-id "q-75982" :question/problem "The matrix $F$ is given below. Let ${f}_{1}$, ${f}_{2}$,  ${f}_{3}$ and ${f}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $F.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(F).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${f}_{4}={x}_{1}{f}_{1}+{x}_{2}{f}_{2}+{x}_{3}{f}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & -24 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $F$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & -24 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(F).$"}]}
